  "purpose": "Development set for upgrade tasks rather than API changes: 20 public repos (12 JavaScript/TypeScript on npm, 8 Python on PyPI) pinned to an end-of-life runtime (Node.js 8 to 20, Python 3.7 to 3.9), an end-of-life framework major (Next.js 12, 13 and 14; Django 2.2 and 4.x) or a deprecated build tool (Create React App), each with a test suite the agent can use to prove its migration. 13 are runtime-eol, 6 framework-major and 1 dependency-major.",
  "criteria": "Curated by hand on 2026-11-06, not recovered from Refit's history. Candidates came from GitHub code search for root .nvmrc files naming Node 14, 16 or 18, root .python-version files naming Python 3.7, 3.8 or 3.9, runtime.txt naming python-3.8, and package.json files naming Next.js 12 or 13 or react-scripts 4; repository search then supplied stars, fork, archived and size. Kept: public, not archived, not a fork, 10 to 5000 stars, under about 50 MB, pushed since mid-2024, not in any other evals/sets/dev/*.json, and a test command (package.json scripts.test, tox, pytest config or a Makefile target) with real test files present. Each repo was shallow-cloned at the default-branch HEAD recorded here (checked against git ls-remote on 2026-11-06), and every declaredIn line was copied from the file at that commit by a script, not retyped. Only files were read: no install, build or test was run, because untrusted repo code may run only in the sandbox and none was available, so every tests.ran is false and no repo is known to pass its own suite today. End-of-life dates were read from the Node.js Release working group's schedule.json and from endoflife.date's product files on GitHub, both pinned to a commit (endoflife.date's own site and API were not reachable from the curation machine); each quote is the exact line for that cycle (the cycle name sits on a nearby line, not in the quote). The Create React App quote is from its own README. The 'to' version is a suggested supported target (Node 24, Python 3.13, Next.js 16, Django 5.2), not something any project announced. Where a repo declares several versions (pyenv lists, CI matrices, tox envlists), 'from' is the version named by the repo's primary declaration (its .nvmrc or .node-version, the default line of .python-version, engines, requires-python or the framework dependency), and the entry's note says where declarations disagree. Repos are ordered by SHA-256 of \"upgrade-dev-20:<owner/name>\" (lower-cased), so a run with a small cap reaches them in an order nobody picked.",
